# Release-path ownership. Every path here can change what a published binary is,
# where it comes from, or who may sign it - so each needs a review from someone
# who holds those rights, not just any writer.
#
# Deliberately NOT a catch-all `*` line: the rest of the tree is ordinary CLI code
# reviewed the ordinary way. Branch protection on `main` requires a code-owner
# review, so a `*` line would make these three people a bottleneck on every change.
.github/workflows/   @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
.github/actions/     @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
.github/CODEOWNERS   @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/release-signing/ @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
scripts/             @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The SET OF PACKAGES that runs inside those same jobs, and the config that decides
# what gets released. Same lever as the scripts above, which is why it belongs at the
# same bar - and it was the one hole in the list:
#
#   - `bun.lock` chooses the code that executes beside the signing identity and the
#     store token. The release lane runs a frozen install and then a publish script
#     that imports `@vercel/blob`; the package manager runs no lifecycle scripts, so
#     a payload has to live in imported code, and imported code is exactly what this
#     file selects.
#   - `package.json`'s `dependencies` are COMPILED INTO THE SHIPPED BINARY by
#     `bun build --compile` (src/api.ts imports zod), then hashed and signed. Nothing
#     is forged in that path - the malicious bytes are genuinely the release - so no
#     amount of job isolation reaches it. Review is the only control, which is why
#     the reviewer has to be the same one who may change a release script.
#   - the release-please files decide the version and the tag that `release-cli.yml`
#     builds from.
#
# Before this, changing a script that runs in the privileged job needed a release
# signer, while changing the set of packages that runs beside it needed one ordinary
# reviewer. That was backwards.
#
# Leading slashes are load-bearing: CODEOWNERS is gitignore-style, so a bare
# `package.json` would match one at any depth rather than the repository root.
/package.json                  @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
/bun.lock                      @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
# `bunfig.toml` carries `minimumReleaseAge` - the floor on how old a version must
# be before it may be pinned at all. It decides nothing on its own (CI installs
# frozen, so the gate never runs there), but it is the stated policy for the two
# files directly above, and lowering it to `0` in a diff that looks like config
# tidying would remove the only thing standing between a bump and an upstream
# release published minutes earlier. Same bar as the files it governs.
/bunfig.toml                   @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
/release-please-config.json    @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
/.release-please-manifest.json @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# THE SELF-UPDATE DECISION PATH. `src/release-signing/` above decides whether a
# signed build record VERIFIES. These two decide what is done with that verdict,
# and no amount of signing reaches them:
#
#   - `src/update.ts` is what a verify failure has to travel through to become a
#     refusal. Widening one error path from "refuse to install" to "warn and
#     continue" defeats the signature check without editing a line of the code
#     that performs it, and it reads like ordinary error-handling polish.
#   - `src/ring-follow.ts` decides WHOSE bytes an install accepts. Self-update
#     compares checksums, not versions, so the recorded pointer is the entire
#     policy for what a machine downloads - and the rule that no record is a
#     REFUSAL rather than a guessed default is the only thing standing between a
#     missing file and an install following a ring nobody chose.
#
# SECURITY.md already declares "the self-update path, including signature
# verification and how an update decides what to install" in scope. Owning only
# the verification half was the disagreement; this closes it.
src/update.ts      @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/ring-follow.ts @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# THE CREDENTIAL PATH, in scope by the same sentence in SECURITY.md:
# "credential handling, the OAuth + PKCE login flow, token storage on disk".
# Each of these holds a control that is invisible to a reviewer who is not
# already looking for it, and each is a plausible-looking small diff away from
# being gone:
#
#   - `src/oauth.ts` performs the token request and receives the access, refresh
#     and id tokens. It is the only place the CLI's credentials exist before
#     something writes them down, and the one client that must stay Auth Code +
#     PKCE (never ROPC).
#   - `src/pkce.ts` generates the verifier and the CSRF `state`. That entropy and
#     the S256 challenge ARE the binding between the browser that authorized and
#     the process that redeems; shortening a length or returning a fixed value on
#     some branch looks like a refactor and makes the flow interceptable.
#   - `src/loopback.ts` decides which callback requests may settle a login -
#     origin first, then `state`. Relaxing either lets any other process on the
#     machine hand the flow an authorization code, or cancel a login in flight.
#   - `src/paste-callback.ts` is a second door onto that same lock: the SSH flow
#     completes by hand through the same interpreter, so what a pasted URL is
#     allowed to be is part of the same decision.
#   - `src/storage.ts` is the 0600 token file. The chmod after the write is there
#     because `mode` applies only on creation; deleting that one line leaves
#     tokens readable by every user on the box, and no test output changes shape.
#   - `src/config-dir.ts` owns the 0700 directory that sits under it. Whichever
#     command runs first on a fresh machine fixes that directory's mode, so a
#     change here can downgrade token storage from a command that never mentions
#     a token.
src/oauth.ts          @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/pkce.ts           @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/loopback.ts       @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/paste-callback.ts @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/storage.ts        @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/config-dir.ts     @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The two that decide WHERE a credential goes and whether it may travel in
# cleartext. Neither is purely a security file, and both are included anyway:
#
#   - `src/target.ts` resolves which backend one binary talks to, at run time,
#     from a flag or an env var. It therefore names the host that receives the
#     access token, and its prod default is a safety property, not a preference:
#     changing the default, or the endpoint bundle a target implies, redirects
#     live credentials without touching any file that mentions a token.
#   - `src/config.ts` carries `assertSecureUrl`, the guard that refuses a
#     plaintext endpoint for anything but a loopback host - the reason auth codes
#     and refresh tokens cannot be sent over `http://`. It is also what
#     `update.ts` uses to check the recorded install URL, so it is the one file
#     on BOTH paths. It does see ordinary config churn; that cost is accepted
#     because the guard is a single `superRefine` away from allowing cleartext
#     everywhere.
src/target.ts @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/config.ts @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# Deliberately NOT owned, so the omissions read as decisions rather than gaps:
#
#   - `src/commands.ts` and `src/index.ts` wire the login flow together, but they
#     also carry every other command in the CLI. Owning them would put a release
#     signer on nearly every pull request in the repository - the bottleneck the
#     missing `*` line exists to avoid. The controls themselves live in the
#     modules above, and those are owned.
#   - The `*.test.ts` files beside these modules. Deleting the test that pins
#     fail-closed buys nothing on its own: the behaviour change it was hiding
#     still has to be made in a file on this list, and that still needs a signer.
